---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/literature/advanced/literary-theory">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced lesson in Literature, and it turns the skills you have practised into
    objects of study. <em>How to Read a Story</em> (foundational) built your first interpretive
    habits; <em>Close Reading</em> and <em>Figures of Speech</em> (intermediate) trained the
    text-facing method this lesson will historicise; and <em>The Novel: A Short History</em>
    (intermediate) gave you the literary history the political lenses will interrogate. Theory
    asks what all that practised reading was assuming — and what a lens lets you find.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>name the major lens families — formalist, structuralist, poststructuralist, political, reader-response — and each one's object;</li>
    <li>run a formalist close reading and say precisely what it refuses to use;</li>
    <li>apply Propp's functions to a tale and explain what "deep structure" claims and omits;</li>
    <li>choose a political lens deliberately and state the question it puts to a text;</li>
    <li>argue why knowing your lens is knowing what you are finding — and what you cannot see.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    You can now close-read with confidence — the intermediate lessons taught figures of speech, form and
    effect. Literary theory asks the question close reading cannot ask about itself: <strong>what assumptions
    make any reading count as a reading?</strong> Every interpretation runs on a lens — about language, about
    authors, about society — usually invisible. Theory's whole century of activity (roughly 1915–2000) was an
    attempt to make the lenses visible, name them, and discover what each one finds. Its core move is simple
    and permanent: <strong>knowing your lens is knowing what you are finding.</strong>
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the major lenses, from text-alone to reader and politics" widget="equationStep" config={theoryCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> reveal the steps one at a time and read each as a
    self-contained manifesto — step 1 narrows the object to the text alone, steps 2 and 3 widen
    it to systems and then to power, and step 4 hands the finishing touch to the reader. The
    order is historical, and each step answers the previous one's blind spot, which is the
    pattern the whole lesson tracks.
  </p>

  <h2>Formalism and New Criticism: the text alone</h2>
  <p>
    The first modern lens was a method, not a doctrine. Russian Formalists like Viktor Shklovsky
    (<strong>ostranenie</strong>, "defamiliarisation": art exists to make the stone stony — to slow perception
    past habit) and the Anglo-American <strong>New Critics</strong> (Cleanth Brooks, <em>The Well Wrought
    Urn</em>, 1947) agreed that the poem is an autonomous verbal object: read the words on the page, their
    irony, paradox and tension, the "organic unity" that resolves them — not the author's biography ("the
    intentional fallacy"), not the reader's feelings ("the affective fallacy"). This gave literary study its
    laboratory method — close reading — and everything since has been a series of expansions of what counts as
    "the text."
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="pair each formalist concept with its claim" widget="match" config={litFormMatch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> formalism means ignoring meaning and history in favour of technique. In
  practice the New Critics read technique as the <em>vehicle of</em> meaning — but their blind spot was real:
  a sonnet written under censorship, they assumed, could be read like one written in freedom. The later
  lenses are, historically, answers to exactly that gap.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the first lens made reading a method and fenced its object — the
    words on the page. The next lens keeps the scientific ambition but changes the object:
    not this poem, but the system that makes poems mean at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Structuralism: literature as a system</h2>
  <p>
    Ferdinand de Saussure's linguistics supplied the model: language is a system of differences — a sign means
    by <em>not being</em> other signs — and the system (langue), not any utterance (parole), is the true
    object. Structuralists imported the move wholesale: Vladimir Propp analysed Russian folktales into
    <strong>31 functions</strong> (absentation, violation, struggle, return...) and seven character roles —
    Cinderella, Bond and Star Wars turn out to share a grammar; Lévi-Strauss found myths organised by binary
    oppositions (raw/cooked, nature/culture); Gérard Genette formalised narration itself (who speaks versus who
    sees; order, duration, frequency). The lens finds the deep code beneath the story — and was then famously
    self-applied: if meaning lives in systems of difference, no system can close.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="file each finding under the code it exposes" widget="sort-buckets" config={litStructBuckets} />

  <h2>Poststructuralism: the system opens</h2>
  <p>
    Roland Barthes's <strong>"The Death of the Author"</strong> (1967) and Jacques Derrida's
    <strong>différance</strong> (meaning endlessly deferred from signifier to signifier, never anchored) form
    the hinge. A text, on this view, is not a vessel carrying an author's intended meaning but a tissue of
    quotations — Julia Kristeva's <strong>intertextuality</strong> — whose readings are never final.
    Derrida's practice, deconstruction, is not (as its detractors assumed) the claim that texts mean nothing;
    it is a disciplined demonstration that a text's hierarchies (speech over writing, presence over absence,
    man over woman) are undermined by the text's own logic. The political consequences follow directly: if
    meaning is not fixed by an author or a canon, the fixed meanings that serve the powerful are
    <em>choices</em> — and can be contested.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="find the line deconstruction would undo" widget="spot-error" config={litPostSpot} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> poststructuralism means any interpretation is as good as any other. Derrida's readings are painstaking textual demonstrations, and the point of "there is nothing outside the text" is that interpretations are argued from evidence within systems — meaning is plural and contested, not arbitrary. The move licenses scrutiny, not anything-goes.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the system opened — meanings defer, authors defer, hierarchies
    undermine themselves. If the fixed meanings that serve the powerful are choices, the next
    question is urgent: whose choices, serving whom? The political lenses take up exactly that.
  </p>

  <h2>The political lenses: whose experience does this serve?</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Marxist criticism</strong> (Eagleton, Raymond Williams) reads literature in its material relations:
    who could publish, who buys books, what ideologies the form naturalises — <em>Jane Eyre</em>'s plot, one
    famous reading notes, requires a woman upstairs and servants downstairs. <strong>Feminist criticism</strong>
    worked in two phases: recovery (Elaine Showalter's <em>A Literature of Their Own</em>, 1977, reconstructed
    the lost tradition of women novelists) and re-reading (Gilbert and Gubar's <em>The Madwoman in the Attic</em>,
    1979, showed the "angel/monster" split women writers were forced into — Bertha Mason is Jane Eyre's
    double); Judith Butler's <em>Gender Trouble</em> (1990) radicalised the lens: gender is performatively
    <em>produced</em> by repeated acts, not expressed — literature is one of the machines of that production.
    <strong>Postcolonial criticism</strong> reads empire from its other side: Said's <em>Orientalism</em>
    (1978) showed the West's textual "East" as a construction that justified rule; Spivak ("Can the Subaltern
    Speak?", 1988) pressed on who can even be heard; Bhabha analysed mimicry and hybridity. The paradigm case
    is Achebe's 1975 lecture on <em>Heart of Darkness</em> — Conrad as "a thoroughgoing racist" whose Africa is
    a prop for a European psychology — a reading that permanently changed how that canonical text is taught,
    not by replacing it but by forcing both lenses into the seminar room. <strong>Queer and critical-race
    approaches</strong> extend the same logic to sexuality and race: the question is always whose experience
    the text makes normal and whose it makes marginal.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="pair each political lens with its case" widget="match" config={litPolyMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the political lenses turned theory outward — from how texts mean
    to whom texts serve. Their mirror image turns inward again, to the last participant the
    century had left out: the reader who completes the text.
  </p>

  <h2>Reader-response: the reader completes the text</h2>
  <p>
    The mirror-image lens: instead of the author, <strong>the reader</strong>. Wolfgang Iser described reading
    as the active filling of <strong>gaps</strong> (a novel never tells us the hero's face); Hans Robert Jauss
    measured works against readers' "horizon of expectations" (how scandalous, how satisfying); Stanley Fish
    went furthest — meaning is produced by <strong>interpretive communities</strong> with shared strategies,
    so the same words compose different poems for different communities, and "the text itself" is the product
    of the reading, not its source. Reader-response explains phenomena formalism cannot: why a 1667 epic
    reads differently in 2026, why jokes die in translation, why the same syllabus text divided a class.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="drag the divider — what the text gives, what the reader brings" widget="compare" config={litReaderCompare} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> reader-response means meaning is whatever a reader privately feels. Iser's gaps are structured by the text, and Fish's communities by shared strategies — readings are constrained and arguable, which is exactly why a class can divide and then argue productively. The lens relocated meaning; it did not abolish standards.</p>
  <p>
    A fourth family, <strong>psychoanalytic criticism</strong> (Freud's dream-work, the Oedipal plot; Lacan's
    mirror stage and the unconscious structured like a language; Northrop Frye's myth-archetype system),
    reads texts as symptom and desire — influential, unfalsifiable, and permanent in criticism's vocabulary.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The lenses in harness, on one short text.</strong> Frost's "The Road Not Taken" is popularly read
    as a celebration of bold individualism. A formalist close reading dissolves that: the poem's two roads are
    "really about the same," worn "really about the same" — and the famous claim that "I took the one less
    traveled by" is spoken by a narrator who admits he will be <em>telling it with a sigh</em>, ages hence —
    a self-flattering story projected onto an undecided choice. The irony the New Critics prized <em>is</em>
    the meaning. A reader-response lens notes the interpretive community that refuses the irony (memes,
    graduation speeches); a Marxist lens asks what a culture wants from this poem that it keeps misreading it
    the same way; a psychoanalytic lens hears regret rehearsing its own alibi. One sixteen-line poem, four
    findings — the lenses do not compete so much as locate different strata. The postcolonial lens widens
    it once more: Frost, the most canonised of American poets, actively cultivated the individualist
    misreading in public readings — the "author function" (Barthes's term) is itself a constructed object,
    and the gap between the biographical showman and the poem's irony reminds us that even the authorial
    context reader-response distrusts is curated material, not ground truth.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="true or false — reading Frost's road" widget="judge" config={litFrostJudge} />

  <h2>Structuralism worked: Cinderella through Propp's functions</h2>
  <p>
    Run the structuralist lens on a tale every culture variants of. Cinderella: the beloved parent's death
    is <strong>absentation</strong> (Propp's function 1); the stepmother's exclusions are
    <strong>interdiction</strong> and <strong>villainy</strong>; the ball invitation is
    <strong>mediation</strong> (the hero learns of what she lacks); the fairy godmother is the
    <strong>magic helper</strong> with the gift; midnight is the <strong>difficulty</strong>; the lost
    slipper is the <strong>mark of recognition</strong>; the fitting is the <strong>solution</strong>;
    the wedding, <strong>return and reward</strong>. Now the structuralist payoff: swap the surface —
    Yeh-Shen's fish-bone helper in the ninth-century Chinese version, Rhodopis and the eagle in the
    ancient Egyptian one, the Ash Lad's successes in Scandinavia — and the <em>function sequence
    persists</em>. Different costumes, same grammar. This is what structuralism means by "deep structure":
    not that all stories are the same, but that a shared generative pattern underlies variations — and its
    limits are equally visible: the lens sees the skeleton and says nothing of the psychologies, the
    histories, the gender politics of who gets to be the magic helper rather than the hero. Every lens,
    structurally blind to something.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="line up Cinderella through Propp's functions" widget="order" config={litProppOrder} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> structuralism claims all stories are the same. Propp's claim is the opposite: surfaces vary infinitely while the function sequence persists — the finding is a shared generative grammar beneath the costumes, and the lens's own admitted blindness (to psychology, history, politics) is precisely what the later lenses supply.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Name the four lens families the widget maps, and state what each takes as its object of study.</li>
    <li>You are handed a Victorian novel in which every woman's property passes through a male relative, and the plot turns on that rule. Which lens do you reach for first, and what is the question it puts to the text?</li>
    <li>Why does the "death of the author" <em>not</em> license the conclusion that any interpretation is as good as any other? Refer to what deconstruction actually does in your answer.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Formalism and New Criticism take the text alone — irony,
    paradox, organic unity; structuralism takes the system — codes, functions, binary
    oppositions; poststructuralism takes the system's instability — deferral, intertextuality;
    the political lenses take the text's social relations — class, gender, empire, sexuality;
    and reader-response takes the reading itself — gaps, horizons, interpretive communities.
    (2) The feminist lens first: the question it puts is whose experience the text makes normal
    — here, an inheritance law that turns women into conduits — and it would read the plot's
    dependence on that rule as ideology working through form, in the mode of Gilbert and
    Gubar's re-readings. A Marxist lens would then ask who profits from the arrangement the
    novel naturalises. (3) Deconstruction is a disciplined practice, not a shrug: it
    demonstrates, from the text's own logic, how its hierarchies undermine themselves — a
    reading that must be argued from textual evidence. "Nothing outside the text" means
    interpretation is argued within systems of difference; meanings multiply, but readings that
    cannot point to evidence are simply bad readings. The move licenses scrutiny, not
    anything-goes.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every interpretation runs on a lens; theory's project was making lenses visible and nameable.</li>
    <li>Formalism gave criticism its method — close reading — and its limitation: a text sealed from history and reader alike.</li>
    <li>Structuralism finds deep codes (Propp's functions, binary oppositions); poststructuralism shows no system closes.</li>
    <li>The political lenses ask whose experience the text serves — and permanently changed what gets taught.</li>
    <li>Reader-response relocates meaning into the reading, constrained by textual gaps and community strategies.</li>
    <li>Lenses do not compete; they locate different strata of the same text — one poem, four findings.</li>
    <li>Every lens is structurally blind to something: knowing your lens is knowing what you cannot see.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Modernism and Postmodernism</em> (advanced) is this subject's other advanced lesson — the
    literary history whose styles and texts the lenses you now own are built to read. For the
    threads this lesson flagged, the expert tier continues with <em>Comparative
    Literature</em> and <em>Textual Criticism and Editing</em> — translation and the scholarly
    edition, the lens beneath all lenses. And if the Cinderella section hooked you, revisit
    <em>World Epics</em> (intermediate) wearing the structuralist lens: oral composition makes
    the deep structure unusually visible.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The theory wars and their settlement.</strong> The 1980s–90s canon arguments — whose books get
    taught — were theory made institutional, and they permanently widened syllabi to women, global and
    minority writers. The current synthesis is pragmatic: most working critics close-read with historically
    informed, politically aware lenses, worn one at a time and named. Two countercurrents matter now.
    Rita Felski's <em>The Limits of Critique</em> (2015) challenges the "hermeneutics of suspicion" — the
    reflex that finds domination under every surface — arguing for postcritical reading that also asks what
    texts <em>do for</em> their readers (attachment, enchantment, recognition). Franco Moretti's
    <strong>distant reading</strong> trades close reading for data across thousands of titles — the rise and
    fall of genres, the novel's spread by decade — a quantitative lens the digital humanities are still
    extending.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="choose a working method — settlement and countercurrents" widget="branch" config={litWarsBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>The unit's other threads plug in here.</strong> Comparative literature and translation studies are
    theory applied across languages: David Damrosch defines world literature as writing that <em>gains in
    translation</em> — circulation, not essence — and every lens above must confront the fact that its
    textual object is a made thing (an edition, a translation, a syllabus selection). Textual criticism is
    the lens beneath all lenses: until you know which words the poet wrote, every reading is provisional —
    the scholarly edition is theory's least glamorous, most indispensable instrument. And the world epics
    (Gilgamesh, the Iliad, the Mahabharata, Sundiata) are the standing test case: oral composition, multiple
    recensions, translation chains — texts that make every assumption of author, origin and fixed meaning
    visibly constructed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Open questions: can computational stylistics <em>adjudicate</em> interpretive disputes, or only complicate
    them? Does the hermeneutics of suspicion exhaust a literature's uses, and what replaces it? And what
    happens to "the author" in an age of machine-generated text — the death of the author announced in 1967
    now has an unfamiliar afterlife. Theory's promise holds: choose your lens knowingly, and you will know
    what you can find — and what, on that lens, you are structurally unable to see.
  </p>

  <p>
    Two newer lenses complete the map for research reading. <strong>Ecocriticism</strong> reads literature's
    relationships with the physical environment — from pastoral's idealisations to climate fiction's
    Anthropocene anxieties — asking how texts model humanity's place in natural systems, and it has grown
    from niche to central as this century's stakes clarified. <strong>Posthumanist criticism</strong>
    interrogates the human-centred assumptions beneath nearly every older lens: who counts as a subject,
    where the boundaries between person, animal and machine run, and what authorship means when it is
    already a distributed assemblage of tools, collaborators and algorithms. Both continue the theory
    wars' underlying motion — each generation enlarging the circle of what criticism must notice — and
    both intersect the digital questions (distant reading, machine-generated text) raised above. The
    historical arc is the discipline itself: formalism taught the close eye, structuralism the system,
    poststructuralism the instability, the political lenses the stakes, reader-response the audience —
    and each addition remains in the working critic's kit rather than replacing its predecessors.
  </p>
